WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.750
Film Courage: Does the antagonist have to
be the strongest character in a movie?

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:07.780
Jason Satterlund, Filmmaker: Hhmm?

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.290
Well that…ohhh?

00:00:09.290 --> 00:00:10.540
Strongest versus not strongest?

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:19.130
I would say they need to be at least as well-defined
as your hero.

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:22.110
I think they need to be one of the strongest
if not the strongest.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.750
Like the hero and the antagonist probably
I would say neck and neck as far as well-defined.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:33.570
Like oftentimes the antagonist is not well-defined
it’s just a villain.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.220
I’ve heard it described as (bad villains)
as Muahaha.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.350
It’s just that they are just evil for the
sake of being evil and that is the most uninteresting

00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:48.890
villain you could create, it’s the bad guy
doing evil, laughing as he beats up somebody.

00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:50.510
C’mon?

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:59.199
What’s more terrifying is…this may be
a terrible example but in the FORCE AWAKENS

00:00:59.199 --> 00:01:03.700
you’ve got Kylo Ren the son of Han Solo
right?

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:06.829
He’s essentially sort of a villain, probably
an anti-hero/villain.

00:01:06.829 --> 00:01:11.750
He’s a complicated character but to me I
found him really terrifying because he’s

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:14.520
like a child with a lot of power.

00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:15.520
That to me is scary.

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.180
He wants to be loved, no one has loved him
before.

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:24.689
That’s the impression I got of him as a
character but boy he can stop a bullet and

00:01:24.689 --> 00:01:27.920
it really makes you afraid of him because
he is so well-developed.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:31.420
I think everyone can identify with that sense
of wanting to be loved.

00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:35.560
I think we’ve all been in the presence with
someone with a lot of power and you don’t

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:41.249
know if they are going to snap at you and
do something dangerous like that.

00:01:41.249 --> 00:01:48.159
Man…that is way more interesting than someone
who is just evil for the sake of being evil.

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.599
The perfect example is the guy, his rival
in the movie.

00:01:52.599 --> 00:01:57.380
Brandon’s son, the redheaded guy who plays,
he the same age.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:02.600
I’m blanking on the name…Gleeson [General
Hux, played by Domhnall Gleeson]!

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.990
He is a little bit more like evil for the
sake of being evil.

00:02:04.990 --> 00:02:11.079
He doesn’t really have the backstory Kylo
Ren so it makes Kylo Ren way more memorable

00:02:11.079 --> 00:02:12.079
in that film.

00:02:12.079 --> 00:02:17.190
Film Courage: You’ve talked about backstory
and The Wound and you’ve compared several

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:21.430
films and said that all of them were orphans?

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:30.280
Jason: Yes, Batman, Spiderman, Harry Potter,
James Bond, Luke Skywalker.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:34.629
The fact that those are all orphans is not
an accident.

00:02:34.629 --> 00:02:35.629
That is on purpose.

00:02:35.629 --> 00:02:40.431
That’s because the idea of not having parents
is like…we can all identify with what that

00:02:40.431 --> 00:02:42.129
might be like.

00:02:42.129 --> 00:02:48.819
Giving your hero a wound, that’s another
way to get us to care about them.

00:02:48.819 --> 00:02:50.980
Orphan is one way but look at Jason Bourne.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:52.719
His wound is amnesia.

00:02:52.719 --> 00:02:59.829
That makes you really go “Man, that’s
a bummer!”

00:02:59.829 --> 00:03:05.099
You make them great at what they do and some
sort of wound that they are dealing with is

00:03:05.099 --> 00:03:08.330
too powerful a narrative things to do.

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.280
Film Courage: And Matt Damon in GOOD WILL
HUNTING he did not have parents either.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:17.269
Jason: That’s right, another orphan and
his wound is from the foster care system.

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:22.299
His wound is this mental anguish that he carries.

00:03:22.299 --> 00:03:27.310
You want him to learn to love so bad in that
movie.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.719
It’s such a brilliantly written film.

00:03:30.719 --> 00:03:32.649
A well-done character.

